<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  CONFIRMED on 2026-08-19, build 12, macOS 27.0 / Apple Silicon.
	
	  SCSIPeripheralsDriverKit requires the SCSI *controller* family entitlement.
	  Apple never minted a peripheral-side key, which is why none appears in
	  their documentation, in the entitlement index, or in any kernel image. The
	  framework simply shares its sibling's.
	
	  Ruled out first, each signed alone and each producing
	  "DK: family entitlements check failed":
	      com.apple.developer.driverkit.family.scsi-peripherals   build 10
	      com.apple.developer.driverkit.family.scsiperipherals    build 11
	
	  With the two keys below the dext starts, matches the enclosure, vends its
	  user client, and reads ESA records with the volume mounted.
	
	  Both are documented and requestable from Apple, so this is a normal
	  entitlement request rather than a dead end:
	      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.developer.driverkit.family.scsicontroller
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.driverkit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.driverkit.family.scsicontroller</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  This one is here to work around a capability that cannot be self-served.

	  The app used to carry com.apple.developer.driverkit.userclient-access,
	  which names this driver and lets that one app, and only that one, open the
	  user client. It is a managed capability ("DriverKit Communicates with
	  Drivers" in the portal), so Apple has to grant it before it can appear in
	  a provisioning profile, and AMFI SIGKILLs a development-signed app that
	  carries an entitlement its profile does not grant.

	  So the permission moves to the driver instead, which is what Apple's own
	  DriverKit sample suggests doing for exactly this reason. The cost is that
	  any app on the machine can now open the user client rather than one named
	  app: looser than we want, acceptable on a development machine, and to be
	  swapped back the day the request is granted. Portal capability:
	  "DriverKit Allow Any UserClient (development)".
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.driverkit.allow-any-userclient-access</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
